@ithline Sure -- the first step would be to clone the repo and set it up for building locally using [the instructions here](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/a3608826a0cf20a1be4a80945c353bfaf6b425e9/docs/BuildFromSource.md). You might run into issues depending on what OS and/or editor you are using and what dependencies are installed.

Assuming that works out for you, you should be able to author a test like the one below in [this file](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/a3608826a0cf20a1be4a80945c353bfaf6b425e9/src/Http/Http.Extensions/test/RequestDelegateGenerator/RequestDelegateCreationTests.Arrays.cs).

```diff
+    [Fact]
+    public async Task NewTest()
+    {
+        var (_, compilation) = await RunGeneratorAsync("""
+app.MapGet("/hello", (int[]? p) => p?.Length);
+""");
+        var endpoint = GetEndpointFromCompilation(compilation);
+
+        var httpContext = CreateHttpContext();
+
+        await endpoint.RequestDelegate(httpContext);
+        // Verify result here
+    }
```

Our generator test infrastructure is set up to run each test case twice, once with the compile-time generator and once with the dynamic run-time code gen so that we can validate the behavior is consistent between the two. Debugging the test should allow you to step through the source generators code and use that to guide dev.
